(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cms-exam-5-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 21-year-old man has mild breathlessness and fatigue two weeks after a viral upper respiratory infection. Troponin is mildly raised, echocardiogram shows mildly reduced function, and he is hemodynamically stable. Which is the mainstay of his treatment?","choices":["Supportive care","Septal ablation","Immediate biopsy","Transplant listing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of myocarditis is directed toward the clinical scenario, and supportive care is the mainstay, with vasodilators, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, diuretics, antiarrhythmics or anticoagulation as needed. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man has had positive blood cultures for 10 days despite an antibiotic regimen that matches his organism's sensitivities, and he still has fevers. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Evaluation for surgery","Stop antibiotics and observe","Start full anticoagulation","Switch to oral therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Infection not resolving with appropriate antibiotic therapy is an indication for surgery, as are heart failure from valve dysfunction, abscess and fungal infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man with a prosthetic aortic valve has endocarditis, and his blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. His son asks how serious this organism is. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["It carries a good prognosis","It rarely needs any surgery","It resolves within a week","It is the most lethal cause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus is the most lethal organism in endocarditis, with mortality highest in prosthetic valve endocarditis (about 50%). — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man with frothy urine and leg swelling is found to have a moderate pericardial effusion. Which tests best evaluate the suspected cause?","choices":["Urine culture","Thyroid function tests","Albumin and urine protein","Antinuclear antibody"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Albumin and urine protein screen for nephrotic syndrome as a cause of pericardial effusion, alongside thyroid, rheumatologic, infectious and malignancy evaluation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with dilated non-ischemic cardiomyopathy, an ejection fraction of 25% and breathlessness on minimal exertion despite optimal medical therapy has a left bundle branch block with a QRS of 0.16 seconds. Which treatment addresses the conduction problem?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Biventricular pacemaker","Single-chamber pacemaker","Catheter ablation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Left bundle branch block is usually left untreated, except symptomatic left bundle branch block with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, which gets a biventricular pacer. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman five days after hip replacement develops sudden breathlessness, pleuritic chest pain and a heart rate of 118. Her electrocardiogram shows a new right bundle branch block. Which acute cause of the block should be suspected?","choices":["Pericardial effusion","Hypertensive crisis","Aortic dissection","Pulmonary embolism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A sudden increase in right ventricular pressure, as from a pulmonary embolism, is a named cause of right bundle branch block, and her postoperative setting fits. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which pair of drugs can cause pericarditis through drug-induced lupus?","choices":["Metoprolol and lisinopril","Colchicine and ibuprofen","Amoxicillin and azithromycin","Procainamide and hydralazine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Iatrogenic causes include trauma, radiation therapy and drug-induced lupus from procainamide and hydralazine, which can produce both pericarditis and pericardial effusion. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a permanent pacemaker has severe claudication, and an intervention is planned. His pacemaker system is an older one, implanted 20 years ago. Which imaging study should be avoided?","choices":["Computed tomography angiography","Duplex ultrasound","Magnetic resonance angiography","Conventional angiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Magnetic resonance angiography is contraindicated with pacemakers and intracranial aneurysm clips, so with his pacemaker it is the study to avoid. It is otherwise the alternative to computed tomography angiography in contrast allergy. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has an asymptomatic ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm that now measures 5.9 cm. Repair is planned. Which approach is usually required for this location?","choices":["Surveillance every 3 to 5 years","Endovascular repair only","Watch until it reaches 6.5 cm","Open repair with root replacement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Surgery is generally indicated for ascending aneurysms over 5.5 cm, and an ascending aneurysm often requires an open surgical approach with aortic root replacement. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with dilated cardiomyopathy returns with recurring ankle edema and increasing abdominal girth. She admits to eating canned soups and fast food most days. Which dietary counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Drink extra fluids with every meal","Restrict sodium to under two grams a day","Raise salt intake to protect pressure","No diet change is needed with drugs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patient education for dilated cardiomyopathy includes sodium restriction to less than two grams a day, along with the medication regimen, follow-up and removing any offending source. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is examined by a student who hears a loud systolic murmur at the left sternal border. Respiration does not change it. Which bedside maneuver would soften her murmur?","choices":["Standing up quickly","Sustained handgrip","Valsalva maneuver","Amyl nitrite inhalation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anything that increases blood flow in the heart reduces outflow obstruction and softens the murmur. Handgrip raises afterload, and squatting or passive leg raising raise preload, so all three decrease the murmur. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman has new atrial fibrillation at 146 with palpitations. She is alert, her blood pressure is 132/84, and she has no chest pain or breathlessness. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Intravenous atropine","Intravenous rate control","Immediate ablation","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A hemodynamically stable patient in atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response gets intravenous rate control; rate control is the first goal. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What may the electrocardiogram show in aortic dissection?","choices":["Always low voltage","Always ST elevation","Diagnostic Q waves","It may be normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram may be normal in aortic dissection, so the diagnosis rests on computed tomography of the chest and abdomen, magnetic resonance imaging or transesophageal echocardiography. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with multiple serious medical conditions is a poor surgical candidate. He had a transient ischemic attack and has a 90% symptomatic carotid stenosis. Which intervention is most appropriate?","choices":["Aspirin and observation alone","Carotid angioplasty with stenting","Carotid endarterectomy","Digital subtraction angiography alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carotid angioplasty with stenting, most often through the common femoral artery, is less invasive and allows intervention in poor surgical candidates with a qualifying stenosis. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 75, 77"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman with dilated, tortuous leg veins has a duplex ultrasound confirming superficial venous reflux. Which vein is most commonly involved in this condition?","choices":["Popliteal vein","Femoral vein","Short saphenous vein","Great saphenous vein"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Varicose veins usually involve the superficial veins, and the great saphenous vein is the most commonly involved, with the short saphenous vein also affected. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has heart-failure-like edema, ascites and an elevated jugular venous pressure. The differential is narrowed to constrictive pericarditis or restrictive cardiomyopathy. Which laboratory finding would favor constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["A markedly high natriuretic peptide","A normal sedimentation rate","A relatively low natriuretic peptide","Positive blood cultures"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. No B-type natriuretic peptide threshold confirms or excludes constriction, but the level is likely lower in constrictive pericarditis than in restrictive cardiomyopathy. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman learns her electrocardiogram shows a constant PR interval of 0.22 seconds with every beat conducted. She feels well and asks at what point this kind of delay usually starts to cause symptoms. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["Once the PR exceeds 0.12 seconds","Once the PR exceeds 0.20 seconds","When a QRS starts dropping","Once the PR exceeds 0.3 seconds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The vast majority of first-degree blocks are asymptomatic; symptoms such as fatigue, dyspnea, palpitations and chest pain usually appear once the PR interval is greater than 0.3 seconds. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with atrial fibrillation is being discharged after treatment of an embolic leg occlusion. Which warning symptoms should prompt him to return immediately?","choices":["Dry, shiny skin on the shins","Mild ankle swelling in the evening","Sudden pain, pallor, coldness, numbness","Calf cramps after long walks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The six P's of arterial assessment (pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, paralysis and poikilothermia) mark acute limb ischemia, a vascular emergency that needs immediate care. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a slow sinus rhythm at 38 six hours after an inferior myocardial infarction. She feels faint and her blood pressure is 80/50. Each narrow QRS has a normal P wave before it. Which drug should be given first?","choices":["Metoprolol","Diltiazem","Atropine","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable sinus bradycardia, here from an infarction affecting the conduction tissue, is treated first with atropine, then a temporary pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with tamponade from a malignant effusion has a blood pressure of 76/48 while the team prepares for pericardiocentesis. Which measure best supports her in the meantime?","choices":["Oral beta blocker now","Sublingual nitroglycerin","Intravenous fluid resuscitation","Intravenous loop diuretic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Intravenous fluid resuscitation increases preload and helps prevent right ventricular collapse; it is a temporizing, stabilizing measure until pericardiocentesis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has a first deep vein thrombosis of the left leg. Genetic testing shows factor V Leiden, an inherited clotting abnormality. Which component of the Virchow triad does this finding represent?","choices":["Venous insufficiency","Hypercoagulable state","Endothelial trauma","Venous stasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Factor V Leiden is an inherited hypercoagulable state, like prothrombin gene mutation and protein C, protein S or antithrombin deficiency. It makes the blood clot too readily, the hypercoagulable component of the triad. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with chest pain after a car accident that killed her sister has ST elevation and an elevated troponin; stress cardiomyopathy is suspected. Her pain has eased and she is hemodynamically stable in the emergency department. Which disposition is most appropriate?","choices":["Discharge home today","Refer for transplant listing","Outpatient follow-up only","Admit to cardiology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Because Takotsubo mimics acute coronary syndrome with no reliable early differences, patients are admitted to cardiology and managed on acute coronary syndrome protocols with supportive treatment. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"In a stable narrow complex tachycardia, which step comes before any drug?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","Synchronized cardioversion","A vagal maneuver","An echocardiogram"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A vagal maneuver is first line for stable supraventricular (narrow complex) tachycardia, before adenosine; synchronized cardioversion is kept for instability. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man has a painful, swollen right groin two days after a cardiac catheterization through the femoral artery. Ultrasound shows a blood-filled cavity outside the arterial wall, communicating with the lumen. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Femoral pseudoaneurysm","Femoral true aneurysm","Acute limb ischemia","Atheroembolism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm is a communication between the arterial lumen and overlying connective tissue after arterial rupture; the common site is the femoral artery after cardiac catheterization, from laceration by the access needle. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Which cardiomyopathy strategy applies specifically to adolescents?","choices":["Palliative care discussions when advanced","Cautious use of diuretics and vasodilators","Assist device or transplant consideration","Genetic screening for hypertrophic disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For adolescents the strategies are genetic screening for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and activity restriction for high-risk individuals. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"In tachy-brady syndrome, which tachyarrhythmia is most common?","choices":["Atrial flutter","Ventricular tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In tachy-brady syndrome a tachyarrhythmia, most commonly atrial fibrillation, alternates with symptomatic bradycardia or with offset pauses. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"For recurrent, colchicine-resistant pericarditis with an inflammatory phenotype, which therapy do the 2025 guidelines now support earlier in the course?","choices":["An interleukin-1 blocker","Immediate pericardiectomy","Intravenous immunoglobulin","Azathioprine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The 2025 guidelines support anti-interleukin-1 therapy (anakinra or rilonacept) earlier in the course with an inflammatory phenotype, and it is preferred for recurrent, colchicine-resistant or steroid-dependent disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 35"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with crushing chest pain develops a wide QRS tachycardia whose complexes vary in shape from beat to beat; his QT before the episode was normal. He is still talking and has a pulse. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Defibrillation","Intravenous amiodarone alone","Observation on the monitor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable, only a short-lived transitional state, so it is defibrillated even when the patient has a pulse and looks well. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with a medial ankle venous ulcer has used compression, elevation and wound care for seven months and the ulcer has not healed. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Vascular specialist","Infectious disease specialist","Dermatologist","Physical therapist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nonhealing ulcers are on the list for referral to a vascular specialist; healing can take 4 to 6 months, and an ulcer still open after that needs further workup for infection or other causes. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 35, 37"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man collapses while playing basketball and arrives with a very fast regular tachycardia. He is alert with a blood pressure of 112/70. A vagal maneuver fails, and adenosine slows the rate to reveal an irregular rhythm with wide QRS complexes, each with a delta wave (slurred upstroke). Which is the next step?","choices":["Oral metoprolol","Intravenous atropine","A second dose of adenosine","Intravenous procainamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When slowing from adenosine reveals atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White, intravenous procainamide is used and adenosine is never repeated: nodal blockade leaves the accessory pathway unopposed. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How many points does an alternative diagnosis at least as likely as deep vein thrombosis subtract in the Wells criteria?","choices":["One point","Two points","Three points","Five points"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An alternative diagnosis at least as likely as deep vein thrombosis scores minus 2, the only negative item; every other item adds 1 point. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman taking a loop diuretic for heart failure has new palpitations felt in her neck. Telemetry shows frequent premature ventricular contractions. Which laboratory values should be checked first?","choices":["Troponin and lipase","Glucose and lactate","Potassium and magnesium","Thyroid hormone and cortisol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Electrolyte imbalances, especially hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia (with hypercalcemia), are main causes of premature ventricular contractions, and a loop diuretic can lower both. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman who smokes and takes an oral contraceptive has mild calf aching. Her Wells score is low, and her D-dimer returns positive. What is the next step?","choices":["Venous duplex ultrasound","Empiric anticoagulation alone","Reassurance and discharge","Repeat D-dimer tomorrow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the low-likelihood pathway a positive D-dimer leads on to ultrasound. The D-dimer is sensitive but not specific, so it cannot confirm a clot, and venous duplex ultrasound has replaced venography for diagnosis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 56, 57 (slide 56 flowchart picture)"},{"q":"Which electrolyte is given as an intravenous push and then as an infusion to break torsades de pointes?","choices":["Magnesium","Phosphate","Potassium","Calcium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium breaks torsades de pointes, and more magnesium is then given as an infusion; it is the main treatment. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man has an incidental finding on a preoperative electrocardiogram: a constant PR interval in conducted beats with occasional P waves that are not followed by a QRS complex, and no PR lengthening before the dropped beats. He has no symptoms. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Reassurance and follow-up","Repeat the tracing in a year","Start an oral beta blocker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type II is considered dangerous even without symptoms, because of unpredictable progression to complete block and a poor prognosis, so the definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has new palpitations. Her pulse is irregularly irregular and the electrocardiogram shows atrial fibrillation. Echocardiogram shows left atrial enlargement. Which treatment prevents embolic stroke in her?","choices":["Diuretic therapy","Nitrate therapy","Anticoagulation","Digoxin for rate control"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is treated with anticoagulation to prevent embolic stroke, together with antiarrhythmics such as amiodarone and, where needed, radiofrequency ablation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which group has the highest incidence of abdominal aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Asian men","Hispanic women","African American women","Caucasian men"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Caucasian men have the highest incidence; aneurysm is uncommon in African American, Asian and Hispanic populations, and men outnumber women 2:1 under 80. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with end-stage dilated cardiomyopathy is listed for cardiac transplantation, but his breathlessness and fatigue worsen despite full medical therapy and a biventricular defibrillator. Which device strategy fits his situation?","choices":["Pacemaker for isolated heart block","Assist device as a bridge to transplant","Alcohol ablation of the septum","Assist device as destination therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A left ventricular assist device pumps blood from the left ventricle into the aorta. As bridge therapy it supports a patient awaiting transplantation or waiting for function to return. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with right lower lobe pneumonia has sharp chest pain. A rubbing sound is heard over the lower chest that is present only while she breathes in and vanishes when she holds her breath. Which is the most likely source of the sound?","choices":["The mitral valve","The pleura","The pericardium","The chest wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pleural rub is tied to respiration, heard in the inspiratory phase, and disappears with breath-holding; a pericardial rub persists, and even intensifies, while the breath is held. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with fever, a new murmur and positive blood cultures is stable. A transthoracic study is limited by his body habitus. Which imaging test best detects vegetations and defines the extent of disease?","choices":["Transesophageal echocardiogram","Chest radiograph","Repeat transthoracic study","Coronary angiography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Transesophageal echocardiography is highly sensitive and specific, detects vegetations on the valve leaflets and defines the extent of disease. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What causes congestive heart failure in infective endocarditis?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation alone","Valvular dysfunction","Fluid from antibiotics","Coronary vasospasm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Congestive heart failure in infective endocarditis is due to valvular dysfunction, and heart failure from valve dysfunction is the most common indication for surgery. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which condition is a contraindication to anticoagulation for deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Extensive proximal clot","Active cancer","A recent long flight","Acute intracranial hemorrhage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulants are contraindicated with active bleeding, acute intracranial hemorrhage, major trauma or severe bleeding disorders, because they would worsen the bleeding. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with peripheral artery disease has a hard, dry, black distal toe with a clear line between healthy and dead tissue. There is no swelling, moisture or fever. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atheroembolism","Wet gangrene","Dry gangrene","Gas gangrene"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dry gangrene has a hard, dry texture, usually involves the distal toes, shows clear demarcation between healthy and necrotic tissue, and is common in patients with peripheral artery disease. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with chronic kidney disease, pulmonary hypertension and hyponatremia is diagnosed with constrictive pericarditis. His family asks what to expect. Which statement about his long-term outlook is most accurate?","choices":["Survival without treatment is usually normal","His age and comorbidities predict a worse outcome","His prognosis is as good as a young patient's","Age does not influence the outcome of surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Older age, worse functional class and comorbidities such as renal dysfunction, pulmonary hypertension and hyponatremia are independent predictors of poor long-term outcome in constriction. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man taking apixaban for deep vein thrombosis develops an acute intracranial hemorrhage. Which agent reverses the anticoagulant?","choices":["Vitamin K by mouth","Andexanet alfa","Idarucizumab","Protamine sulfate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Andexanet alfa reverses the direct factor Xa inhibitors apixaban and rivaroxaban in life-threatening or intracranial bleeding, and it is also used off-label for edoxaban. Hemorrhage is the most serious adverse effect of anticoagulation. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which valve-related condition warrants endocarditis prophylaxis before dental work?","choices":["A history of rheumatic fever","A pacemaker without valve disease","A transcatheter prosthetic valve","Sinus rhythm with a flow murmur"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prosthetic cardiac valves, including transcatheter-implanted prostheses and homografts, and prosthetic valve-repair material warrant prophylaxis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with a native mitral valve has 5 weeks of low-grade fever, fatigue and a new murmur. Three sets of blood cultures have just been drawn, and the organism is not yet known. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone","Oral amoxicillin, single dose","Nafcillin plus rifampin","Oral doxycycline for 2 weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy covers staphylococci, streptococci and enterococci; vancomycin plus ceftriaxone is the regimen for a subacute presentation, native valve endocarditis, or a prosthetic valve in place more than a year. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with restrictive cardiomyopathy is found to have cardiac amyloidosis, with amyloid protein deposited in the heart tissue. He has breathlessness, leg edema and ascites. Which electrocardiogram finding is expected with this infiltrative disease?","choices":["ST elevation","Left axis deviation","Low QRS voltage","Prominent Q waves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram in restrictive cardiomyopathy may show low QRS voltage in amyloidosis infiltrative disease, and atrial fibrillation and bundle branch blocks are also common. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has frequent, disruptive episodes of atrioventricular nodal reentry and wants a definitive cure. Referral for which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Septal myectomy","Permanent pacemaker","Valve replacement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation offers a select subset of patients with nodal reentry the opportunity for a definitive cure, in over 95%. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman collapses in a hospital hallway. The monitor shows chaotic, uncoordinated activity, and she has no pulse. Compressions have started and defibrillator pads are attached. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Check electrolytes first","Amiodarone before any shock","Synchronized cardioversion","Defibrillate now"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ventricular fibrillation is treated as cardiac arrest with cardiopulmonary resuscitation, oxygen and defibrillation (unsynchronized cardioversion) as soon as possible. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man began having pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) pericardial pain 8 weeks ago. Despite treatment the symptoms have never fully gone away, with no symptom-free interval. Which term best describes his course?","choices":["Incessant pericarditis","Effusive-constrictive disease","Chronic pericarditis","Recurrent pericarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms persisting beyond 4 to 6 weeks and up to 3 months without a sustained remission define incessant pericarditis; past 3 months the label becomes chronic. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 3-week-old infant born at 28 weeks is in the neonatal intensive care unit with a central venous catheter and parenteral nutrition. She develops feeding difficulty, tachycardia, hypotension and a new murmur, and echocardiography shows a right-sided vegetation. Which organism group is particularly tied to her risk factors?","choices":["Gram-negative coccobacilli","Viridans streptococci","Candida species","Enterococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neonatal fungal endocarditis is tied to central venous catheters, hyperalimentation and neonatal intensive care or prematurity; Staphylococcus aureus and coagulase-negative staphylococci are also common. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man ran out of his metoprolol four days ago. He feels his heart racing, and his pulse is 118 and regular, with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. He has no fever and is not bleeding. Which is the most likely cause of his tachycardia?","choices":["Raised intracranial pressure","Hypothyroidism","Increased vagal tone","Beta blocker withdrawal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Abrupt withdrawal of a beta blocker (or a nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker) is a listed cause of sinus tachycardia, which fits stopping metoprolol four days ago. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"An 82-year-old woman taking digoxin for heart failure has nausea and fatigue. Her heart rate is 48 and regular, with narrow QRS complexes and retrograde P waves (inverted) just after each QRS in II, III and aVF. Which is the most likely cause of this rhythm?","choices":["Digoxin toxicity","Hyperthyroidism","An accessory pathway","Missed digoxin doses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A slow regular narrow rhythm with inverted P waves after the QRS is a junctional rhythm, and digoxin toxicity is a listed cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has been in the hospital for 9 days after hip surgery. She develops a new fever, and two sets of blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus; she has no indwelling catheter, and examination finds no wound or other source. Which diagnosis must be suspected?","choices":["Contamination of both culture sets","Postoperative atelectasis","Health care-associated endocarditis","Drug fever from antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Health care-associated endocarditis should be suspected in hospitalized patients who develop fevers and positive blood cultures without an apparent source. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has been hospitalized for four days receiving intravenous antibiotics through a peripheral catheter in her left forearm. She now has pain, redness and warmth along the course of the vein, and a firm, tender, palpable cord can be felt beneath the skin. She is afebrile, and the redness stays within the line of the vein. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cellulitis","Upper extremity deep vein thrombosis","Septic thrombophlebitis","Superficial thrombophlebitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tenderness, induration and erythema along a superficial vein, with an indurated palpable cord after recent catheter use, is superficial thrombophlebitis. Redness confined to the vein argues against infection. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has had painful blue toes for several days after atheroemboli from proximal plaques. Which complication may follow in the affected digits?","choices":["Digital necrosis and gangrene","Rupture of the popliteal artery","Hoarseness from nerve stretch","Heart block from nodal fibrosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Digital vascular occlusion from atheroemboli causes ischemia and blue toe syndrome, and digital necrosis and gangrene may develop in the affected toes. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman being evaluated for endocarditis had a soft systolic murmur documented for 10 years. Today the murmur sounds a little louder. How does this change count under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["It is an immunologic phenomenon","It does not meet the major criterion","It confirms definite endocarditis","It meets the major criterion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. New valvular regurgitation counts as endocardial involvement, but an increase or change in a preexisting murmur is not sufficient. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man underwent cardiac catheterization through the right femoral artery and vein ten days ago. He now has a continuous machine-like murmur over the puncture site with a palpable thrill (a vibration felt over the skin), and his right leg is swollen with prominent superficial veins. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Deep vein thrombosis of the leg","Iatrogenic arteriovenous fistula","Superficial thrombophlebitis","Femoral pseudoaneurysm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Femoral puncture for percutaneous access can connect the artery and vein, and the bruit and thrill with venous congestion of the leg follow. Lower extremity fistulas are most commonly iatrogenic from groin access. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man had an anterior myocardial infarction 3 weeks ago. He now has fever, pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) chest pain eased by sitting forward, and a friction rub (a scratchy, three-part rubbing sound) at the left sternal border. Which drug should be avoided in treating his pericarditis?","choices":["Ibuprofen","Colchicine","Pantoprazole","Aspirin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This is post-infarction (Dressler) pericarditis, and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs such as ibuprofen are not given after a myocardial infarction; glucocorticoids are also avoided. Aspirin plus colchicine is used. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"}]};
